📂 Project Workflow

1. Data Preparation

* Load the Arabic news dataset.
* Clean and normalize Arabic text.
* Remove unwanted characters and noise.
* Tokenize articles and headlines.
* Build the vocabulary.
* Convert text into numerical sequences.
* Apply padding to ensure consistent sequence lengths.
* Prepare decoder input and target sequences.

2. SimpleRNN Seq2Seq Model

Build an Encoder–Decoder architecture using:

* Embedding Layer
* SimpleRNN Encoder
* SimpleRNN Decoder
* Dense Layer with Softmax activation

The Encoder processes the input article and passes its hidden state to the Decoder, which generates the headline word by word.

3. LSTM Seq2Seq Model

Build a second Encoder–Decoder architecture using LSTM layers.

LSTM uses hidden states and cell states to help capture long-term dependencies and retain important information from longer sequences.

4. Model Training

Both models are trained using the same prepared dataset.

The training process includes:

* Training and validation data.
* Teacher Forcing.
* Loss monitoring.
* Training and validation loss visualization.

5. Headline Generation

The trained models generate Arabic headlines for unseen articles.

The generation process starts with a special start token and continues word by word until the end token is generated or a maximum sequence length is reached.

6. Model Evaluation & Comparison

Compare SimpleRNN and LSTM based on:

* Training Loss.
* Validation Loss.
* Training time.
* Generated headline quality.
* Ability to produce relevant and coherent headlines.

⸻

🧠 Model Architecture

SimpleRNN Encoder–Decoder

The SimpleRNN Encoder reads the article sequence and produces a context representation. The Decoder uses this representation to generate the headline sequentially.

LSTM Encoder–Decoder

The LSTM Encoder processes the article and passes its internal states to the Decoder. The LSTM architecture is designed to better handle longer-term dependencies in sequential data.

